      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions",
      "problem": "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{1+x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The Maclaurin series for $\\frac{1}{1-x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-{x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} & =1+(-{x}^{2})+{(-{x}^{2})}^{2}+{(-{x}^{2})}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{6}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, replacing $x$ with $-x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+x} & =1+(-x)+(-x)^{2}+(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the expansions for $\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{1+x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{1+x} & =(1-{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{6}+⋯)+(1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}-⋯) \\\\  & =(1+1)+(-x)+(-1+1){x}^{2}+(-{x}^{3})+(1+1){x}^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =2-x-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
